[23:06:38] ═══ OVERALL SUMMARY
[23:06:38]       SRP test outcome: NO_SRP_SIGNATURE
[23:06:38]       Because cos(D) and cos(D)/r_sun^2 are 99.97% collinear over Earth's e = 0.0167 orbit (VIF = 1808), radial-bin scaling tests bound only anomalously steep 1/r^2 dependence rather than the 3.3% annual modulation of standard orbital SRP. Unlike EPM21, INPOP (17a/19a/21a) and DE430 do not integrate lunar orbital SRP in the equations of motion, instead applying a -3.65 mm SRP + 0.65 mm thermal = -3.00 +/- 0.50 mm cos(D) correction a posteriori (Viswanathan 2017; Williams et al. 2009; Ke et al. 2026). Forward time-domain subtraction of the full [-3.65 (1 AU / r_sun)^2 + 0.65] mm cos(D) waveform reduces the precision-weighted full-systematic static C1 amplitude from -5.18 +/- 0.73 mm (7.08 sigma) to -2.18 +/- 0.89 mm (2.46 sigma combined; -2.71 +/- 1.07 mm, 2.53 sigma in the pure 3rd-order true-ecliptic harmonic basis D_ecl of Ke et al. 2026, -2.72 +/- 1.08 mm, 2.51 sigma with annual and monthly terms, or -4.23 +/- 1.07 mm, 3.94 sigma on the mean Delaunay polynomial D_del) and absorbs 91.1% of the static isotropic intercept in the joint kinematic-dipole Model E (eta_0 from -2.54e-4 to -0.23e-4, i.e., C1,0 from -3.30 +/- 0.79 mm to -0.29 +/- 0.94 mm combined, 0.31 sigma), while leaving the SRP-orthogonal radial-velocity quadrature term eta_vr (t = +6.50, p = 7.9e-11) and fixed-sky orientation term eta_theta (t = -11.02, dipole split Delta_eta = -1.72e-3 at 9.19 sigma cleaned; -1.76e-3 at 7.92 sigma full archive) 100.0% invariant.
